course: dictionary
lesson: 12726
title: Draggled: a picture word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Draggled» is an old-fashioned word that paints a picture. Look at the clues around it, such as rain, mud or a river. Those clues tell you that the character has been through something wet and messy.
explain: In Alice in Wonderland, the creatures climb out of the Pool of Tears as «a queer-looking party», with draggled feathers and fur clinging close. Their feathers are not neat and dry. They are soggy and limp, so draggled must mean wet and messy.
explain: Because draggled is an old word, you may not hear it often today. When you meet it, swap in a simpler word, such as wet and messy. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Draggled means wet, muddy and untidy, often after being dragged through mud or rain.
fact: «Draggled» can also mean hanging limp and dirty, like a long skirt or a bird's wet feathers.
fact: Draggle is a verb too: draggles, draggled, draggling. It is old-fashioned, so you will mostly find it in older books.
fact: «Draggled and dirty» is an example of a picture-making word that helps readers see a character clearly.
fact: The word sounds like "drag", which can help you remember that something dragged along the ground gets messy.
exercise: «The puppy came in from the rain with draggled fur.» What does draggled mean? | Sleepy and warm | Clean and fluffy | Loud and happy | *Wet, muddy and untidy
exercise: «Her long coat was draggled after she fell in the river.» Which clue shows the coat got wet? | her long coat | draggled | *fell in the river | was
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to draggled? | *messy | tall | bright | happy
test: «The dog's draggled tail trailed in the mud.» What does draggled mean here? | Tidy and brushed | Dry and shiny | Loud and bouncy | *Wet, muddy and limp
test: Which word is the best swap for draggled in «draggled and dirty»? | Warm | *Muddy and untidy | Sparkling | Neat
exam: «After the storm, the birds stood on the bank, their feathers draggled and dripping.» Why does the author choose draggled? | To tell us the time of day | *To paint a picture of wet, messy birds | To show the birds are sleeping | To say the birds are very tall
